WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.952
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:12.300
Hey, everybody.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.660
Welcome back to the
Data Science Hangout.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:18.330
It's so nice to see
everybody today.

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:21.180
If this is your first
time joining us today--

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:24.170
I know there was a lot of
love for Matt on LinkedIn,

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:25.610
so we might have
some people here

00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:27.800
joining for the very first time.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:29.280
Thank you for joining us.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.479
This is an open space for the
whole data science community

00:00:32.479 --> 00:00:36.020
to connect and chat about data
science leadership, questions

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:39.020
you're facing, and getting to
learn about what's going on

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:41.810
in the world of data science
across different industries

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:43.100
and companies.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.110
So the sessions are
recorded, and they're

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:49.460
shared to the Posit on YouTube,
as well as our Data Science

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:50.270
Hangout site.

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:53.030
So you can always go
back and rewatch or find

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:55.010
helpful resources.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:57.470
Together at the Data
Science Hangouts,

00:00:57.470 --> 00:01:00.440
we're dedicated to creating
a welcoming environment

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:03.380
for everybody, so we love
when you all can participate

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:07.340
and we can hear from everyone,
no matter your area of work

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:10.760
or level of experience too.

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:15.140
There's always multiple ways
you can ask questions-- and not

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:17.820
just ask questions, but also
provide your perspective.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.780
So if there's something you
want to jump in and weigh in on,

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:23.720
you can jump in by raising
your hand on Zoom here.

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:27.455
You can put questions or
comments into the Zoom chat.

00:01:27.455 --> 00:01:29.330
And feel free to put a
little star next to it

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:31.310
if you want me to read it too.

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:34.220
I see a lot happening
in the chat right now.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:36.770
But I could also call on you
to introduce yourselves and add

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:38.540
some context too.

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:42.530
We also have a Slido link
where you can ask questions

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:45.650
anonymously, and our
team will put that

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:48.930
into the chat in
just a second here.

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:51.480
But with all of that,
thanks again for joining.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:55.760
I'm so excited to be joined by
my co-host today, Matt Dancho,

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:58.130
founder of Business Science.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:01:59.390
And we were just talking.

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:03.350
Matt and I spoke at
the RStudio Conference

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:04.885
back in July about the hangouts.

00:02:04.885 --> 00:02:07.010
We were like, let's get
something on the books,

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:09.229
and here we finally are.

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:11.120
So thanks for joining, Matt.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:13.632
I'd love to have you introduce
yourself and maybe share

00:02:13.632 --> 00:02:14.465
a little bit about--

00:02:14.465 --> 00:02:15.393
MATT DANCHO: Sure.

00:02:15.393 --> 00:02:17.060
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: --the
work you do and--

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:18.320
Yeah, yeah.

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:23.730
Well, first off, Rachael,
thanks so much for having me.

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:27.510
We were chatting
and we had talked

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:31.230
about doing this back in July,
which was when the RStudio

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:32.910
Conference was going on.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:35.940
And like you said, things
have just flown by,

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:37.470
and here we are in November.

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:39.280
And this is exciting.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:43.650
I'm excited to be here, so
thank you so much for having me.

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:46.090
Just to introduce
myself, my name's Matt.

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:50.110
If you guys haven't ever met me
before, it's great to meet you.

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:53.100
Thanks so much for showing up.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:56.070
I don't know what
your background is.

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:59.040
It sounds like there might
be beginners, experts,

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.230
maybe a mix here.

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:02.490
So do me a favor.

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:06.630
If you're a beginner, can you
put just a three in the chat?

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:08.700
Just put the number
three so I can

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:10.290
get a sense of where we're at.

00:03:13.347 --> 00:03:14.680
All right, all right, all right.

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:15.250
OK.

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:18.460
And if you're an expert,
put a five in the chat.

00:03:21.315 --> 00:03:21.940
Robert, five.

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:24.065
MATT DANCHO: All right, 4
and 1/2, 4 and 1/2, yeah.

00:03:24.065 --> 00:03:27.820
[LAUGHING] All right, cool, so
we've got a good mix of people.

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:32.140
So I like to ask this
just so I know how to--

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:35.320
before I start diving into
what I do and everything,

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:38.590
just to get a general
consensus on--

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:42.460
wow, we got a negative five--

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:44.890
just to see where you
guys are at skill-wise.

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:48.760
And the cool thing is, no
matter where you're coming from,

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:49.930
you're going to learn stuff.

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:50.810
We're here.

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:51.310
We're open.

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:56.980
Like Rachael said, we're open,
so feel free to ask questions.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:58.430
Little bit about myself--

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:00.280
my name is Matt Dancho.

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:05.420
I've been doing R for about--

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:09.500
I want to say 2013 was like
officially my moment when I--

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:14.000
yeah, I'm going for
into this thing--

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:16.777
which, back then it was
just statistics or--

00:04:16.777 --> 00:04:19.069
it wasn't even I don't think
quite called data science.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:28.250
You guys probably can
identify with this.

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:32.570
I got bit by the R bug bad.

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:35.090
I became obsessed,
and I just started

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:37.070
like learning as much
as I possibly could

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:39.440
and trying to apply
it to my day job

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:41.870
at the time, which I was
working for a small company

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:43.960
called Bonney Forge.

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:50.290
And I was just helping them
handle their customer data,

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:54.400
work with the
manufacturing team,

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:58.750
understand-- back then
there was a big drop in oil

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:01.570
prices and the
products that we made

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:06.250
went into oil, so I was doing
a lot of forecasting and things

00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:07.430
of that nature.

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:13.120
So fast forward to today,
in 2018, I got into--

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:15.910
2017, 2018, I got into
consulting kind of as a side

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:19.360
hustle at first, and then it
slowly became my full-time job.

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:24.730
And then in 2018, I
pivoted into education,

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:29.230
because that's where I really
saw a huge demand for--

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:30.970
as I was doing a lot
of these consulting

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:34.690
projects for companies in the
marketing domain or finance

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:39.160
domain, it was clear to me that
the problem wasn't that they

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:40.330
needed a consultant.

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:43.700
They really needed education
and they needed help.

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:46.390
So in 2018 I founded
Business Science.

00:05:46.390 --> 00:05:48.130
So that's my company.

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:52.540
And since then, I've been
building a lot of courses.

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:54.760
That's what the first
four years were.

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:56.890
And now we're
getting to the point

00:05:56.890 --> 00:05:58.930
where we're starting to
add additional programs

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:01.450
and scale up the programs too.

00:06:01.450 --> 00:06:06.550
So now we have about, I think,
4,000 or 5,000 students,

00:06:06.550 --> 00:06:10.780
and in 2018, we
started with one.

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:13.630
So it's been kind
of a wild ride.

00:06:13.630 --> 00:06:16.420
Are any of you guys students--

00:06:16.420 --> 00:06:19.030
Business Science students
on the call today?

00:06:19.030 --> 00:06:20.260
Put a one in the chat.

00:06:22.936 --> 00:06:23.830
Javier, yeah.

00:06:23.830 --> 00:06:25.884
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Oh, I see some.

00:06:25.884 --> 00:06:26.509
That's awesome.

00:06:26.509 --> 00:06:27.759
MATT DANCHO: All right, sweet.

00:06:27.759 --> 00:06:28.330
Sweet.

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:31.030
So what was really cool
back in RStudio Conference--

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:33.280
I got to meet a
ton of my students.

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:38.090
I met, I think, over 30 students
of mine, and it's really cool.

00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:41.320
I got pictures with them,
got to talk with them,

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:42.830
got to see them face to face.

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:44.590
And I had no clue,
I didn't even think

00:06:44.590 --> 00:06:46.298
I was going to run
into a single student.

00:06:51.190 --> 00:06:55.750
For me it's a humbling moment,
because I see business science

00:06:55.750 --> 00:06:58.370
education is getting out there
and it's having an impact,

00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:02.390
and those are the cool
things that I like to see.

00:07:02.390 --> 00:07:02.890
All right.

00:07:02.890 --> 00:07:05.350
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Absolutely.

00:07:05.350 --> 00:07:05.890
Yeah.

00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:08.140
MATT DANCHO: I probably went
a little deeper than what

00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:09.582
you may have wanted, but--

00:07:09.582 --> 00:07:10.540
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Sorry.

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:11.915
We had a little--
here, I'm going

00:07:11.915 --> 00:07:16.880
to ask RStudio co-host to help
with the mute buttons here.

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:18.680
Let's see.

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:20.260
There we go.

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:21.730
Thanks, everybody.

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:24.580
I do see some questions
already coming in, Matt.

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:26.800
And this is one
that I actually was

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:29.140
really interested
to ask you about as

00:07:29.140 --> 00:07:32.320
well, your journey
going from your company

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:34.540
to starting your own business.

00:07:34.540 --> 00:07:38.290
And the question says,
Matt, I'm really eager.

00:07:38.290 --> 00:07:39.940
Could you take us
through your journey

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:42.100
to becoming a mini
celeb in the space?

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:43.100
MATT DANCHO: Oh my gosh.

00:07:43.100 --> 00:07:46.707
Was it scary to go off
and do your own thing?

00:07:46.707 --> 00:07:47.540
MATT DANCHO: It was.

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:55.200
So I'll talk a little bit
about what was going on.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:08:00.170
So the tough part for me
was giving up a six-figure--

00:08:00.170 --> 00:08:05.630
a cushy six-figure job,
salary to do something new

00:08:05.630 --> 00:08:08.420
and take on a risk.

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:11.090
And I think, as an
entrepreneur, it's something

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:14.690
that I've always wanted to
do, but you never really know

00:08:14.690 --> 00:08:15.995
when the timing is right.

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:25.895
In 2017, I had started slowly--

00:08:25.895 --> 00:08:28.160
I made the decision to
build my first course,

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:30.810
and I didn't know what
it was going to do.

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:33.679
I launched it in 2018.

00:08:33.679 --> 00:08:39.380
I think it was April or May of
2018, and I got 50 people in it

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:41.570
really quick.

00:08:41.570 --> 00:08:44.030
That to me was a huge
signal that there

00:08:44.030 --> 00:08:45.650
was an actual demand.

00:08:45.650 --> 00:08:47.960
Was it enough to
offset my salary

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:52.740
and feel like, oh, yeah,
I'm going to do it?

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:54.600
No, not at all.

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:57.725
So some things happened
later throughout that year.

00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:04.910
I want to say maybe middle of
2018, so like August of 2018,

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:09.500
I ended up making the
decision to leave my company,

00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:12.260
and again, give
up that cushy job.

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:15.030
It was a huge risk
at that point.

00:09:15.030 --> 00:09:19.190
And I remember even
throughout that year--

00:09:19.190 --> 00:09:21.710
I remember in
December of that year,

00:09:21.710 --> 00:09:26.810
I think I only made like
$3,000 or $4,000 that month.

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:28.700
And this is coming
from somebody who

00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:31.310
was making a lot,
probably three or four

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:34.200
times that amount every month.

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:36.410
And there was definitely
some question marks,

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:40.947
like am I going to be
able to pull this off?

00:09:40.947 --> 00:09:44.480
But for me, the
things that really

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:48.290
help when I decided to
actually take that step forward

00:09:48.290 --> 00:09:51.800
was sitting down,
looking at the numbers,

00:09:51.800 --> 00:09:54.870
figuring out what people
want, talking to customers,

00:09:54.870 --> 00:09:57.410
talking to my
students at that time,

00:09:57.410 --> 00:10:01.820
figuring out what their
needs were, and then really

00:10:01.820 --> 00:10:03.800
just giving it my all.

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:06.210
I looked at the numbers
and I saw that, hey,

00:10:06.210 --> 00:10:10.850
if we could do this
amount each month,

00:10:10.850 --> 00:10:13.810
we're going to be
able to be fine.

00:10:13.810 --> 00:10:16.435
My wife, she was still working
full time at that point.

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:25.030
That for me was like, OK--

00:10:25.030 --> 00:10:29.080
January of 2019,
I put my head down

00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:33.130
and just started working
and building courses, and--

00:10:33.130 --> 00:10:37.450
after reviewing the numbers,
and got the confidence in myself

00:10:37.450 --> 00:10:39.940
to really be able to do it.

00:10:39.940 --> 00:10:43.080
And I just started doing it.

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:44.330
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: That's great.

00:10:44.330 --> 00:10:47.480
And something that we've
talked about a few times

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:51.050
on past weeks of the
hangout is on building

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:53.870
your personal brand, and I
think that's something that you

00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:56.270
do a really great job of.

00:10:56.270 --> 00:10:58.772
I know we originally met
on LinkedIn, I think.

00:10:58.772 --> 00:10:59.480
MATT DANCHO: Yep.

00:10:59.480 --> 00:11:01.580
And I was just
curious, for people

00:11:01.580 --> 00:11:04.730
who are jumping into this
space too and looking

00:11:04.730 --> 00:11:09.000
to build their personal brand,
do you have any tips for us?

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:11.400
MATT DANCHO: Yeah, absolutely.

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:14.347
So Rachael, building
your personal brand--

00:11:14.347 --> 00:11:16.430
and you know this, because
you've done a fantastic

00:11:16.430 --> 00:11:18.380
job as well,
especially with being

00:11:18.380 --> 00:11:21.680
part of RStudio and Pause--

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:23.420
I think you're doing
a fantastic job--

00:11:23.690 --> 00:11:24.190
--at this.

00:11:24.190 --> 00:11:28.830
But everyone can really benefit
from building a personal brand.

00:11:28.830 --> 00:11:31.100
It doesn't matter
if you're trying

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:34.880
to get a job, if you're trying
to build your own company,

00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:38.780
if you're trying to do--

00:11:38.780 --> 00:11:42.560
even just grow yourself
personally and step out

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:43.700
of your comfort zone.

00:11:43.700 --> 00:11:45.890
I think it's really
important for everyone

00:11:45.890 --> 00:11:48.440
to build their own brand.

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:52.190
Some tips I can give you--

00:11:52.190 --> 00:11:56.160
don't feel like you
need to get it perfect.

00:11:56.160 --> 00:11:59.340
There's a guy that
I follow on Twitter.

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:01.740
His name is Justin Welsh.

00:12:01.740 --> 00:12:03.540
And he does a
really good job at--

00:12:03.540 --> 00:12:07.110
for people who are trying to
start their own business, how

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:10.680
to do your own personal brand.

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:13.470
Some of the value that
I've gotten from him--

00:12:13.470 --> 00:12:15.810
talking about your journey.

00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:19.560
No matter where you
are, just every day,

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:21.660
just going on
LinkedIn, or maybe--

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:24.100
make it a point two
or three times a week.

00:12:24.100 --> 00:12:25.710
Just put a post out
there of something

00:12:25.710 --> 00:12:30.220
new that you've learned or
something you're interested in.

00:12:30.220 --> 00:12:33.390
And it doesn't have to be a
whole production, because this

00:12:33.390 --> 00:12:34.800
is the mistake
that I always made

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:39.820
was I always felt like my
copy has to be super on point

00:12:39.820 --> 00:12:44.220
and it needs to be something
I've thought about for days.

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:47.580
And while that
stuff does work, I

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:50.820
think what is more
valuable is being authentic

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:54.820
and just kind of showing
like, hey, I'm not perfect.

00:12:54.820 --> 00:12:55.680
I learned something.

00:12:55.680 --> 00:12:57.490
I'm excited about it.

00:12:57.490 --> 00:13:00.000
I've learned this new R package.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.370
People identify with
that, and then you

00:13:02.370 --> 00:13:04.530
start to grow your followers.

00:13:04.530 --> 00:13:07.590
And that can lead to
new job opportunities.

00:13:07.590 --> 00:13:12.030
That can lead to never know who
that connection's going to be.

00:13:12.030 --> 00:13:14.550
And they may be the person
that helps you, because we all

00:13:14.550 --> 00:13:16.800
need help, right?

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:21.740
No matter where you are,
you're not going to get--

00:13:21.740 --> 00:13:26.180
you're not going to be the
best version of yourself

00:13:26.180 --> 00:13:28.530
without getting a little
bit of help along the way.

00:13:28.530 --> 00:13:32.840
And I think using LinkedIn
and using Twitter to that,

00:13:32.840 --> 00:13:36.390
and just posting a little bit
about your journey every--

00:13:36.390 --> 00:13:39.170
doesn't have to be every day--

00:13:39.170 --> 00:13:40.920
of times each week really--

00:13:40.920 --> 00:13:43.595
that'll make a huge difference.

00:13:43.595 --> 00:13:44.720
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Thank you.

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:45.637
That's really helpful.

00:13:48.007 --> 00:13:49.840
I'm starting to see
some questions coming in

00:13:49.840 --> 00:13:50.860
through the chat too.

00:13:50.860 --> 00:13:52.900
So Jeremy, I see you
just asked a question.

00:13:52.900 --> 00:13:54.730
Do you want to jump
in live and ask it?

00:13:59.810 --> 00:14:02.150
I see you unmuted, but I'm
not sure I can hear you yet.

00:14:02.150 --> 00:14:02.540
Yeah.

00:14:02.540 --> 00:14:03.320
RACHAEL DEMPSEY:
Oh, there you are.

00:14:03.320 --> 00:14:03.820
Hey.

00:14:03.820 --> 00:14:04.940
I'm just interested.

00:14:04.940 --> 00:14:08.630
I do some online teaching, and
I do a lot of meetings online.

00:14:08.630 --> 00:14:09.990
I'm still working from home.

00:14:09.990 --> 00:14:12.671
So I'm just interested
in how you--

00:14:12.671 --> 00:14:17.090
what technology you use to
get the best experience when

00:14:17.090 --> 00:14:19.280
you're connecting
with people remotely--

00:14:19.280 --> 00:14:20.570
hardware and software.

00:14:20.570 --> 00:14:26.270
And how do you integrate
R into these technologies?

00:14:26.270 --> 00:14:27.295
MATT DANCHO: OK, OK.

00:14:30.020 --> 00:14:32.970
Great question, Jeremy.

00:14:32.970 --> 00:14:36.150
The main types of
softwares that I use--

00:14:36.150 --> 00:14:37.620
I mean, Zoom--

00:14:37.620 --> 00:14:42.540
I do a webinar once every--

00:14:42.540 --> 00:14:43.860
I was doing it every two weeks.

00:14:43.860 --> 00:14:45.840
Now I'm going to
do it every week.

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:47.670
But I usually try
to do a webinar,

00:14:47.670 --> 00:14:55.260
and I try to teach a little
bit about R and certain topics.

00:14:55.260 --> 00:14:58.030
One that I'm actually getting
ready to teach is time series.

00:14:58.030 --> 00:14:59.610
I'm going to be
doing that next week.

00:14:59.610 --> 00:15:01.770
But every week or
every couple of weeks,

00:15:01.770 --> 00:15:07.320
I try to get on a
Zoom webinar with--

00:15:07.320 --> 00:15:10.320
I just open it up to
everybody, because it's

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:12.280
kind of a way to give back.

00:15:12.280 --> 00:15:17.220
It's something that I'm very
interested in helping people

00:15:17.220 --> 00:15:22.680
grow their R experience, because
I think too, oftentimes, you

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:25.920
see people out there and
they just don't know about R,

00:15:25.920 --> 00:15:30.750
or they may be on the edge
of, should I do data science?

00:15:30.750 --> 00:15:32.370
Could I do data science?

00:15:32.370 --> 00:15:33.900
I'm working in Excel right now.

00:15:33.900 --> 00:15:38.010
I'm doing Power BI, but
what else can I do--

00:15:38.010 --> 00:15:39.840
be doing to grow my career?

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:42.210
So doing a webinar--

00:15:42.210 --> 00:15:46.887
so Zoom for webinars
is fantastic.

00:15:46.887 --> 00:15:47.970
Trying to think what else.

00:15:47.970 --> 00:15:52.710
Obviously, I use the RStudio
product line, or the Posit

00:15:52.710 --> 00:15:54.150
product line, but RStudio--

00:15:54.150 --> 00:15:57.300
the IDE is fantastic.

00:15:57.300 --> 00:16:03.840
Shiny-- I think that is such
a misunderstood technology

00:16:03.840 --> 00:16:08.370
out there that people
kind of often overlook.

00:16:08.370 --> 00:16:12.570
So I love showcasing
Shiny as a way

00:16:12.570 --> 00:16:16.200
to really take R into
production, because everyone

00:16:16.200 --> 00:16:21.900
always talks about MLOps and
putting things into production.

00:16:21.900 --> 00:16:23.550
How do you do that with R?

00:16:23.550 --> 00:16:24.900
Guys, it's right there.

00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:26.850
It's Shiny.

00:16:26.850 --> 00:16:28.350
It's fantastic.

00:16:28.350 --> 00:16:30.100
And let's see.

00:16:30.100 --> 00:16:30.600
What else?

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:31.100
What else?

00:16:31.100 --> 00:16:33.560
What other
technologies do I use?

00:16:33.560 --> 00:16:35.740
I mean, through my
business, I probably

00:16:35.740 --> 00:16:39.130
touch 30 or 40
different technologies.

00:16:39.130 --> 00:16:41.110
And we could probably
talk all day about those.

00:16:41.110 --> 00:16:43.027
But do you have anything
specific, Jeremy,

00:16:43.027 --> 00:16:44.110
that you're interested in?

00:16:49.160 --> 00:16:52.370
JEREMY SKOG: No, I'm just
interested in hearing about how

00:16:52.370 --> 00:16:56.690
you keep people engaged who've
been in meetings all day

00:16:56.690 --> 00:17:00.890
and then tune in to
another Zoom meet-up.

00:17:00.890 --> 00:17:01.985
Yeah.

00:17:01.985 --> 00:17:04.910
OK, this is good,
because something

00:17:04.910 --> 00:17:06.920
I started working
with is actually

00:17:06.920 --> 00:17:11.579
a coach and an a
marketing guy as well.

00:17:11.579 --> 00:17:14.630
And that's really helped me
in my presentation skills,

00:17:14.630 --> 00:17:17.450
because in the online
age, it's really tough

00:17:17.450 --> 00:17:20.099
to capture people's engagement.

00:17:20.099 --> 00:17:23.420
So I don't know how
deep, Rachael, you

00:17:23.420 --> 00:17:26.859
want to go into the
art of presentations.

00:17:26.859 --> 00:17:28.609
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Let's
dive in, whatever--

00:17:28.880 --> 00:17:30.110
--everybody wants
to task about.

00:17:30.110 --> 00:17:30.620
MATT DANCHO: All right.

00:17:30.620 --> 00:17:31.537
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Yeah.

00:17:31.537 --> 00:17:34.910
If you guys want
to go, I'll go.

00:17:34.910 --> 00:17:36.620
All right.

00:17:36.620 --> 00:17:41.180
When you do a
presentation, people

00:17:41.180 --> 00:17:43.010
are on Zoom calls all day.

00:17:43.010 --> 00:17:45.830
With COVID and everything,
the last thing you want to do

00:17:45.830 --> 00:17:48.320
is you want to just
get on to a Zoom call

00:17:48.320 --> 00:17:50.125
and just talk at
people for 60 minutes.

00:17:50.125 --> 00:17:52.250
And that's the last thing
that they want to do too,

00:17:52.250 --> 00:17:54.448
because they're going to be--

00:17:54.448 --> 00:17:56.990
after the first three minutes,
they're going to tune you out,

00:17:56.990 --> 00:17:57.620
right?

00:17:57.620 --> 00:18:01.400
So what we started doing
on our marketing webinars

00:18:01.400 --> 00:18:04.487
and on our technical webinars
is really just trying

00:18:04.487 --> 00:18:05.570
to keep the engagement up.

00:18:05.570 --> 00:18:09.320
So what I did here with like
the threes and the fives,

00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:11.330
that's a way to engage.

00:18:11.330 --> 00:18:13.880
So you just ask a
question to the audience,

00:18:13.880 --> 00:18:17.837
and oftentimes, that keeps
them kind of in there

00:18:17.837 --> 00:18:18.545
that much longer.

00:18:24.120 --> 00:18:28.160
I have a note on my keyboard--

00:18:28.160 --> 00:18:31.130
don't be boring.

00:18:31.130 --> 00:18:33.830
And so I will do weird stuff.

00:18:36.650 --> 00:18:40.840
I've started to play around
with the way that I talk.

00:18:40.840 --> 00:18:45.100
So I used to talk just like
I would conversationally,

00:18:45.100 --> 00:18:48.820
but in a webinar you want to
talk a little bit differently.

00:18:48.820 --> 00:18:52.720
You want to actually accentuate
your speech patterns.

00:18:52.720 --> 00:18:57.250
So if you have kind
of a long sentence--

00:18:57.250 --> 00:18:59.710
I'm talking about
RStudio, how great it is,

00:18:59.710 --> 00:19:01.810
and I want to make
sure that you get

00:19:01.810 --> 00:19:05.290
the most important
information today,

00:19:05.290 --> 00:19:08.440
and these are the three things
I want you to walk away with.

00:19:08.440 --> 00:19:13.400
So I'll play around with
my speech a little bit.

00:19:13.400 --> 00:19:17.471
Those inflection points
will keep people interested.

00:19:17.471 --> 00:19:21.350
And also, starting everything
with a big promise,

00:19:21.350 --> 00:19:24.030
like, hey, why are
you here today?

00:19:24.030 --> 00:19:25.530
What are you going
to get out of it?

00:19:25.530 --> 00:19:27.440
So I started doing
this big promise

00:19:27.440 --> 00:19:29.900
at the beginning of my webinars.

00:19:29.900 --> 00:19:32.192
And I just started
saying, hey, I'm

00:19:32.192 --> 00:19:33.650
going to teach you
one thing today.

00:19:33.650 --> 00:19:36.108
If you don't walk away from
this presentation with anything

00:19:36.108 --> 00:19:38.420
else, this is what
you're going to do today.

00:19:38.420 --> 00:19:42.365
And for my time series
presentation or for--

00:19:42.365 --> 00:19:45.810
I just did one on
techs analysis.

00:19:45.810 --> 00:19:47.570
It was one skill
that they're going

00:19:47.570 --> 00:19:52.070
to learn that is really going
to allow them to improve

00:19:52.070 --> 00:19:54.650
their model performance
and potentially

00:19:54.650 --> 00:19:57.650
save their company up
to a million dollars.

00:19:57.650 --> 00:20:00.530
So it's something big that
they're going to walk away,

00:20:00.530 --> 00:20:04.610
and they're going to see at
the end how we accomplish that.

00:20:04.610 --> 00:20:06.890
I think you do a
couple of those things,

00:20:06.890 --> 00:20:11.243
and you're going to see that
your Zoom calls, your webinars

00:20:11.243 --> 00:20:13.160
that you're on-- especially
if you're training

00:20:13.160 --> 00:20:16.310
data scientists,
some of that stuff

00:20:16.310 --> 00:20:17.705
can get dull pretty quickly.

00:20:20.495 --> 00:20:23.000
I often think that
the more that they can

00:20:23.000 --> 00:20:25.730
identify with you as a person--

00:20:25.730 --> 00:20:28.460
so I'll mix in a
little bit of my story,

00:20:28.460 --> 00:20:31.620
usually in the
beginning of my talks.

00:20:31.620 --> 00:20:35.160
Especially for new people that
aren't accustomed to you yet,

00:20:35.160 --> 00:20:38.963
I think that that
makes a big difference.

00:20:38.963 --> 00:20:40.130
So that was a good question.

00:20:40.130 --> 00:20:40.880
RACHAEL DEMPSEY:
Yeah, great question.

00:20:40.880 --> 00:20:41.963
JEREMY SKOG: That's great.

00:20:41.963 --> 00:20:43.880
Thank you.

00:20:43.880 --> 00:20:45.680
That's really helpful, Matt.

00:20:45.680 --> 00:20:48.620
I've been thinking a lot
about a lot of the communities

00:20:48.620 --> 00:20:51.470
that people build within
their own companies too,

00:20:51.470 --> 00:20:54.980
and getting people engaged
and interested in those.

00:20:54.980 --> 00:20:58.070
That's really helpful
snippet for us.

00:20:58.070 --> 00:20:58.820
MATT DANCHO: Yeah.

00:20:58.820 --> 00:21:00.980
And I think case studies too--

00:21:00.980 --> 00:21:02.840
people often in companies--

00:21:02.840 --> 00:21:04.940
what I saw was,
even though you're

00:21:04.940 --> 00:21:08.262
on the same team,
a lot of times you

00:21:08.262 --> 00:21:09.470
don't see what they're doing.

00:21:09.470 --> 00:21:12.560
So sharing just a case
study or an example of how

00:21:12.560 --> 00:21:15.200
you're using R in that
company can oftentimes

00:21:15.200 --> 00:21:17.780
help grow R within--

00:21:17.780 --> 00:21:21.410
inside the walls, because
you might be in the marketing

00:21:21.410 --> 00:21:22.850
group, but the
manufacturing group

00:21:22.850 --> 00:21:26.645
may have a similar situation
that they don't even

00:21:26.645 --> 00:21:27.770
know that it can help them.

00:21:27.770 --> 00:21:28.853
So yeah.

00:21:28.853 --> 00:21:30.020
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Absolutely.

00:21:30.020 --> 00:21:30.812
MATT DANCHO: Share.

00:21:30.812 --> 00:21:31.670
Tell stories.

00:21:31.670 --> 00:21:33.290
Talk about your case studies.

00:21:33.290 --> 00:21:36.062
All that's really good stuff.

00:21:36.062 --> 00:21:37.520
RACHAEL DEMPSEY:
Marlene, I see you

00:21:37.520 --> 00:21:38.820
asked a question in the chat.

00:21:38.820 --> 00:21:41.450
Do you want to jump in?

00:21:41.450 --> 00:21:41.950
Sure.

00:21:41.950 --> 00:21:43.180
Hey, Matt.

00:21:43.180 --> 00:21:44.380
Good morning or afternoon.

00:21:44.380 --> 00:21:46.780
I don't know what time
zone people are in.

00:21:46.780 --> 00:21:50.980
I'm in Key West right now,
so I'm East Coast time zone.

00:21:50.980 --> 00:21:54.508
It is just noon-- just hit noon.

00:21:54.508 --> 00:21:55.300
MARLENE HANKEN: OK.

00:21:55.300 --> 00:21:57.250
MATT DANCHO: Nice
to see you, Marlene.

00:21:57.250 --> 00:22:00.010
We just had the time shift,
so I'm super discombobulated.

00:22:00.010 --> 00:22:03.312
People on the call
were [INAUDIBLE] me.

00:22:03.312 --> 00:22:11.140
My question was around starting
to socialize data sharing,

00:22:11.140 --> 00:22:13.665
and it's a struggle for
certain organizations.

00:22:13.665 --> 00:22:15.790
And I'm just wondering if
you've handled that a bit

00:22:15.790 --> 00:22:17.290
as a consultant.

00:22:17.290 --> 00:22:21.430
Obviously, you deal a lot with
the real hands-on data work,

00:22:21.430 --> 00:22:24.760
but how do you start to
address, or like I said,

00:22:24.760 --> 00:22:28.030
socialize the concept of
breaking down some silos

00:22:28.030 --> 00:22:30.595
and barriers to data sharing?

00:22:30.595 --> 00:22:32.160
I don't know if you
can just go, hey,

00:22:32.160 --> 00:22:36.110
it's as easy as RStudio Connect
or whatever you recommend, or--

00:22:36.110 --> 00:22:36.610
I don't how.

00:22:36.610 --> 00:22:39.925
How do you start having
that conversation?

00:22:39.925 --> 00:22:43.300
So I'm a big fan of
including as many stakeholders

00:22:43.300 --> 00:22:45.760
throughout the
project as possible.

00:22:45.760 --> 00:22:50.020
So a typical example might be,
if I was doing a consulting

00:22:50.020 --> 00:22:54.010
project with one of the
companies I work with back

00:22:54.010 --> 00:22:57.760
in the day, like S&amp;amp;P
Global, MRM//McCann.

00:22:57.760 --> 00:22:59.290
They're a big marketing firm.

00:22:59.290 --> 00:23:01.780
S&amp;amp;P Global, they're
in the finance.

00:23:01.780 --> 00:23:05.560
So I normally have
a point of contact

00:23:05.560 --> 00:23:07.070
there that I'm working with.

00:23:07.070 --> 00:23:13.810
But what I'll try and do is,
as we come up with the project

00:23:13.810 --> 00:23:17.200
discovery phase, I'll ask
a lot of questions like,

00:23:17.200 --> 00:23:19.720
what are your pain points?

00:23:19.720 --> 00:23:21.490
How does this affect
your organization?

00:23:21.490 --> 00:23:25.630
Really try to go a couple
layers deep with them.

00:23:25.630 --> 00:23:28.750
And what ends up happening
is they'll be like, oh, yeah,

00:23:28.750 --> 00:23:30.790
well, this affects this
part of the company.

00:23:30.790 --> 00:23:32.530
It's like, well,
should we involve them

00:23:32.530 --> 00:23:34.090
in the conversation?

00:23:34.090 --> 00:23:37.150
And they're like, yeah, it's
probably not a bad idea.

00:23:37.150 --> 00:23:40.940
So you start building
a group of people.

00:23:40.940 --> 00:23:43.540
And then once you get
them all filled in

00:23:43.540 --> 00:23:46.060
on what you guys
are trying to do,

00:23:46.060 --> 00:23:48.760
that often just helps
break down the silos,

00:23:48.760 --> 00:23:52.840
and they can understand
and talk in an open forum

00:23:52.840 --> 00:23:57.400
where everyone's equal, and just
really try to understand this--

00:23:57.400 --> 00:24:02.200
whatever that problem is,
because most of the time,

00:24:02.200 --> 00:24:04.300
the data science team--

00:24:04.300 --> 00:24:07.060
you're kind of acting
like a consultant.

00:24:07.060 --> 00:24:09.010
A lot of times,
my impression of--

00:24:09.010 --> 00:24:12.250
is they're almost doing
a consulting capacity

00:24:12.250 --> 00:24:14.030
inside of an organization.

00:24:14.030 --> 00:24:17.530
So the toughest part is
to be able to get everyone

00:24:17.530 --> 00:24:21.460
comfortable so that, number one,
they give you access to data.

00:24:21.460 --> 00:24:24.040
But number two, get--

00:24:24.040 --> 00:24:25.780
just get them talking
and communicating

00:24:25.780 --> 00:24:32.020
so that way you really
can break down those--

00:24:32.020 --> 00:24:36.227
I don't know if they're
silos or barriers-- whatever

00:24:36.227 --> 00:24:37.060
you want to call it.

00:24:37.060 --> 00:24:39.730
But you've got to break this
down so everyone's talking,

00:24:39.730 --> 00:24:43.132
and then that's going to make
them more comfortable for sure.

00:24:43.132 --> 00:24:44.090
MARLENE HANKEN: Got it.

00:24:44.090 --> 00:24:45.560
Yeah, I appreciate that.

00:24:45.560 --> 00:24:48.410
It's a struggle, and
you hit the nail right

00:24:48.410 --> 00:24:51.580
on the head in operating on
kind of like a consultancy.

00:24:51.580 --> 00:24:52.580
That's what I feel like.

00:24:52.580 --> 00:24:55.250
I am running around to about--

00:24:55.250 --> 00:24:57.260
we're a family of
companies, six companies.

00:24:57.260 --> 00:25:01.100
So I'm calling each of the
companies saying, what's

00:25:01.100 --> 00:25:02.840
top of the priority list?

00:25:02.840 --> 00:25:05.870
What are you trying to
automate and/or work towards?

00:25:05.870 --> 00:25:07.910
I'm on a five- or
six-year plan to get us

00:25:07.910 --> 00:25:10.917
into machine learning, so it's
going to be a long haul still.

00:25:10.917 --> 00:25:11.750
But I appreciate it.

00:25:11.750 --> 00:25:15.180
What company do you work with,
if you don't mind me asking?

00:25:15.180 --> 00:25:15.680
Sure.

00:25:15.680 --> 00:25:17.000
I work for Western Growers.

00:25:17.000 --> 00:25:22.040
We're a trade association for
specialty crops growers, so not

00:25:22.040 --> 00:25:24.480
corn and not
[INAUDIBLE] of those.

00:25:24.480 --> 00:25:27.500
So basically, if you enjoy leafy
greens, anybody on the call,

00:25:27.500 --> 00:25:29.930
we take care of the growers
that leafy greens all

00:25:29.930 --> 00:25:31.100
over the country.

00:25:31.100 --> 00:25:33.440
90% of your leafy
greens in California.

00:25:33.440 --> 00:25:34.010
Yeah.

00:25:34.010 --> 00:25:35.285
Thank you for [INAUDIBLE].

00:25:35.285 --> 00:25:36.560
[LAUGHS]

00:25:36.560 --> 00:25:41.250
MATT DANCHO: Yeah, I mean, it's
not going to happen overnight,

00:25:41.250 --> 00:25:43.850
so I'm glad you guys
have an actual plan

00:25:43.850 --> 00:25:45.247
that you guys are working to--

00:25:45.247 --> 00:25:46.580
although you might be surprised.

00:25:46.580 --> 00:25:49.617
You can get it done probably
faster than five years,

00:25:49.617 --> 00:25:51.950
depending on how large your
organization is and how much

00:25:51.950 --> 00:25:54.830
of that what-- do they call it--

00:25:54.830 --> 00:25:58.220
corporate inertia, so to speak.

00:25:58.220 --> 00:26:01.670
But yeah, and the technology
will play a big role too.

00:26:01.670 --> 00:26:03.080
So you mentioned
RStudio Connect.

00:26:03.080 --> 00:26:05.540
