00:27:44.220 --> 00:27:46.710
what does that mean for the
way you design your courses,

00:27:46.710 --> 00:27:51.870
for the way you pitch what
the company does for people

00:27:51.870 --> 00:27:53.130
and that sort of thing?

00:27:53.130 --> 00:27:56.298
How is that a day-to-day part
of the identity of the company?

00:27:56.298 --> 00:27:57.090
MATT DANCHO: Right.

00:27:57.090 --> 00:27:58.923
That's a good question,
Alan, and I'm really

00:27:58.923 --> 00:28:00.340
glad you brought that up.

00:28:00.340 --> 00:28:03.990
So the identity of my
company Business Science

00:28:03.990 --> 00:28:07.480
has actually evolved over time.

00:28:07.480 --> 00:28:10.140
Initially, I was just trying
to combine business and data

00:28:10.140 --> 00:28:14.220
science in a way,
and I'm a very--

00:28:14.220 --> 00:28:18.810
I want to be-- it to be
obvious what the naming

00:28:18.810 --> 00:28:19.510
of the company--

00:28:19.510 --> 00:28:21.120
I'm not like a
company like Apple,

00:28:21.120 --> 00:28:23.850
where I'm going to
call myself Apple

00:28:23.850 --> 00:28:26.100
and then it's going to be
a technology company that

00:28:26.100 --> 00:28:29.380
builds phones, and computers,
and those sorts of things,

00:28:29.380 --> 00:28:33.090
and all the other
stuff that they do now.

00:28:33.090 --> 00:28:36.600
That's originally why I
called it Business Science.

00:28:36.600 --> 00:28:39.100
And again, my roots
were in consulting,

00:28:39.100 --> 00:28:43.590
so I was actually applying
data science to business,

00:28:43.590 --> 00:28:45.210
and that's really--

00:28:45.210 --> 00:28:47.550
was the crux of what I
was trying to go after.

00:28:47.550 --> 00:28:49.800
And when I was coming up
with the training initially,

00:28:49.800 --> 00:28:53.760
that's really what I
wanted to emphasize

00:28:53.760 --> 00:28:58.740
is a business-forward or a
business-first type of way

00:28:58.740 --> 00:29:00.540
of learning data science.

00:29:00.540 --> 00:29:03.840
Now, with that said,
things have changed

00:29:03.840 --> 00:29:06.540
a lot in the past four
years, or five years

00:29:06.540 --> 00:29:08.100
that I've been
running this thing.

00:29:08.100 --> 00:29:11.820
And I will say this.

00:29:11.820 --> 00:29:15.570
Seriously, I actually view--

00:29:15.570 --> 00:29:19.710
and we can get at a really
deep level of conversation

00:29:19.710 --> 00:29:26.040
on this, but I really view
that data scientists and data

00:29:26.040 --> 00:29:27.060
analysts--

00:29:27.060 --> 00:29:29.310
I think that there's going
to be a new roll here

00:29:29.310 --> 00:29:32.160
in the next couple of years
that companies are going

00:29:32.160 --> 00:29:35.790
to start adopting the role
of the business scientist,

00:29:35.790 --> 00:29:37.830
because you already
have a business analyst.

00:29:37.830 --> 00:29:40.920
And I haven't really talked
a whole lot about this yet,

00:29:40.920 --> 00:29:46.170
but the more I think about it,
the problem and the challenge

00:29:46.170 --> 00:29:49.050
with data science and
why companies often

00:29:49.050 --> 00:29:52.212
fail with data science,
it's not because they have--

00:29:52.212 --> 00:29:53.670
they don't have
good people or they

00:29:53.670 --> 00:29:57.670
don't have problem
solvers or this or that,

00:29:57.670 --> 00:30:03.960
but they lose sight of the
business-first mentality, where

00:30:03.960 --> 00:30:06.480
you need to be able to
start with a problem

00:30:06.480 --> 00:30:08.190
in an organization,
be able to work

00:30:08.190 --> 00:30:10.800
with many different levels
of the organization,

00:30:10.800 --> 00:30:13.590
from executives
to line managers,

00:30:13.590 --> 00:30:15.865
the people who are
doing the work.

00:30:15.865 --> 00:30:17.490
And you have to work
cross-functionally

00:30:17.490 --> 00:30:21.390
and cross-sectional
throughout the company.

00:30:21.390 --> 00:30:27.130
And that to me requires a heavy
business acumen, not just like,

00:30:27.130 --> 00:30:29.940
hey, I know data, and
I know a few algorithms

00:30:29.940 --> 00:30:32.220
that I can throw at the data.

00:30:32.220 --> 00:30:35.040
No, that's not how it goes.

00:30:35.040 --> 00:30:37.860
The most successful
people that I've seen

00:30:37.860 --> 00:30:40.620
are the ones that are able
to communicate effectively,

00:30:40.620 --> 00:30:43.800
that know what the target
is of the organization,

00:30:43.800 --> 00:30:47.550
the objectives, and they can
translate that into all the

00:30:47.550 --> 00:30:48.930
different--

00:30:48.930 --> 00:30:52.260
they can take that
down and translate it

00:30:52.260 --> 00:30:54.270
across the organization.

00:30:57.060 --> 00:30:59.490
The more and more that
I think about this,

00:30:59.490 --> 00:31:01.950
I really view that there is--
probably in the next couple

00:31:01.950 --> 00:31:06.870
of years, you're going to
start seeing actual roles on--

00:31:06.870 --> 00:31:10.260
within companies on Indeed
for the business scientists.

00:31:10.260 --> 00:31:13.770
And I'm going to start
promoting that here actually--

00:31:13.770 --> 00:31:20.100
start in 2023 you'll see more
of me talking about that.

00:31:20.100 --> 00:31:20.962
So good question.

00:31:20.962 --> 00:31:22.170
ALAN SCHUSSMAN: That's great.

00:31:22.170 --> 00:31:22.670
Thanks.

00:31:22.670 --> 00:31:25.566
I love hearing the
evolution and your--

00:31:25.566 --> 00:31:28.350
the way that what you're
seeing in the industry

00:31:28.350 --> 00:31:32.580
and in companies is informing
