00:38:59.440 --> 00:39:00.180
Put a six.

00:39:00.180 --> 00:39:02.260
We'll do a six.

00:39:02.260 --> 00:39:02.760
All right.

00:39:02.760 --> 00:39:06.120
All right, so Juan, you're
seeing the chat, right?

00:39:06.120 --> 00:39:06.810
Yes.

00:39:06.810 --> 00:39:10.080
Lots of people are having
imposter syndrome too,

00:39:10.080 --> 00:39:12.000
so that's the first thing.

00:39:12.000 --> 00:39:14.970
I would say probably
90% of data scientists

00:39:14.970 --> 00:39:18.690
experience some level
of imposter syndrome.

00:39:18.690 --> 00:39:20.220
It's very common.

00:39:20.220 --> 00:39:22.320
I experienced it.

00:39:22.320 --> 00:39:24.540
I'll share with you my
story and some of the things

00:39:24.540 --> 00:39:26.710
that I did to get over it.

00:39:26.710 --> 00:39:30.030
But my problem was--

00:39:30.030 --> 00:39:33.900
so I'm a data
scientist, self-trained.

00:39:33.900 --> 00:39:36.810
I never went to school
for data science.

00:39:36.810 --> 00:39:39.690
I was a mechanical engineer.

00:39:39.690 --> 00:39:42.870
I worked in the
valve industry mainly

00:39:42.870 --> 00:39:49.080
doing actually design work
and maintenance efforts,

00:39:49.080 --> 00:39:55.170
and then managing a actual
team of engineers that did--

00:39:55.170 --> 00:39:58.710
that built valves, basically.

00:39:58.710 --> 00:40:02.160
I eventually got
into the sales group,

00:40:02.160 --> 00:40:06.900
because they liked what
I was doing managerially

00:40:06.900 --> 00:40:08.580
with that engineering group.

00:40:08.580 --> 00:40:10.770
So I got some additional
responsibility,

00:40:10.770 --> 00:40:14.340
and I started to work my
way up in the organization.

00:40:14.340 --> 00:40:22.960
But I started learning data
science, and it wasn't easy.

00:40:22.960 --> 00:40:26.320
It was far from it.

00:40:26.320 --> 00:40:32.430
Honestly, it took me probably
at least four or five years

00:40:32.430 --> 00:40:36.000
to think of myself as an
actual data scientist.

00:40:36.000 --> 00:40:38.700
And there were some days
when I was going through it.

00:40:38.700 --> 00:40:41.970
And I remember trying
to make reports,

00:40:41.970 --> 00:40:44.520
and just nothing's
coming together.

00:40:44.520 --> 00:40:45.630
I'm drawing blanks.

00:40:45.630 --> 00:40:48.570
I'm just staring at the screen.

00:40:48.570 --> 00:40:50.490
The cursor's blinking at me.

00:40:50.490 --> 00:40:56.950
And I'm just like,
Matt, pull it together.

00:40:56.950 --> 00:41:00.790
What the heck's wrong?

00:41:00.790 --> 00:41:06.830
So people go through that--
and I venture to say at least 9

00:41:06.830 --> 00:41:09.100
out of 10 data scientists.

00:41:09.100 --> 00:41:11.000
It's pretty common.

00:41:11.000 --> 00:41:15.530
So things that can help,
things that help me--

00:41:15.530 --> 00:41:16.930
don't give up.

00:41:16.930 --> 00:41:19.960
Every day is a new day, so try
and write at least one line

00:41:19.960 --> 00:41:20.650
of code a day.

00:41:20.650 --> 00:41:22.570
That was something
that I implemented

00:41:22.570 --> 00:41:25.720
in my own personal journey,
and I did that for--

00:41:25.720 --> 00:41:28.510
I don't know-- seven or
eight years straight.

00:41:28.510 --> 00:41:31.750
And that single
activity of saying, hey,

00:41:31.750 --> 00:41:34.840
I got to pull up
RStudio, I got to start--

00:41:34.840 --> 00:41:36.880
I just got to put one
line of code in there.

00:41:36.880 --> 00:41:38.380
And you know what's
going to happen?

00:41:38.380 --> 00:41:39.838
It's a mental thing,
because you're

00:41:39.838 --> 00:41:42.160
going to write more
than one line of code.

00:41:42.160 --> 00:41:44.140
Who's going to stop at
one line of code, right?

00:41:44.140 --> 00:41:47.270
I mean, it takes you--

00:41:47.270 --> 00:41:48.910
so anyways, I
started doing that.

00:41:48.910 --> 00:41:51.940
I started being
consistent with my habits.

00:41:51.940 --> 00:41:54.910
I started really doubling
down on some of the things

00:41:54.910 --> 00:41:58.900
that I was learning ggplot,
just getting visualizations.

00:41:58.900 --> 00:41:59.800
Those are quick wins.

00:41:59.800 --> 00:42:01.900
Once I learned
ggplot, I could then

00:42:01.900 --> 00:42:04.120
start telling stories
to my management team.

00:42:04.120 --> 00:42:08.810
And that was something that
nobody else in my company

00:42:08.810 --> 00:42:09.310
was doing.

00:42:09.310 --> 00:42:11.155
So I started focusing
on things like that

00:42:11.155 --> 00:42:14.977
that weren't necessarily
overly complex,

00:42:14.977 --> 00:42:16.810
like machine learning
or anything like that.

00:42:16.810 --> 00:42:19.690
I set some of that stuff
aside and really focused

00:42:19.690 --> 00:42:21.760
on the foundational things.

00:42:21.760 --> 00:42:23.710
I got really good
with data wrangling.

00:42:23.710 --> 00:42:26.650
That was another area where--

00:42:26.650 --> 00:42:30.382
people in my company had
tough times when there was--

00:42:30.382 --> 00:42:31.840
especially in a
SQL database, where

00:42:31.840 --> 00:42:34.510
you've got a jillion tables.

00:42:34.510 --> 00:42:35.950
Which ones do you pull in?

00:42:35.950 --> 00:42:39.010
So I learned one table,
the customer table.

00:42:39.010 --> 00:42:41.770
I learned the
transactions table.

00:42:41.770 --> 00:42:47.010
I learned how to connect the
two and start to blend the data.

00:42:47.010 --> 00:42:50.500
And this is stuff that people
had a really tough time

00:42:50.500 --> 00:42:52.450
with in my company,
and it was something

00:42:52.450 --> 00:42:53.890
that differentiated me.

00:42:53.890 --> 00:42:56.050
And again, just a quick win--

00:42:56.050 --> 00:42:59.260
you start to learn a
handful of dplyr functions,

00:42:59.260 --> 00:43:01.700
like group_by and summarize.

00:43:01.700 --> 00:43:05.620
How often is that
exactly the one-two punch

00:43:05.620 --> 00:43:10.150
that your boss needs in order
to be able to gain insights?

00:43:10.150 --> 00:43:13.765
So I really focused
on some of those.

00:43:16.720 --> 00:43:20.750
One of my biggest mistakes
is I would get on LinkedIn,

00:43:20.750 --> 00:43:23.180
and honestly, I
would see everyone

00:43:23.180 --> 00:43:25.640
talking about deep learning.

00:43:25.640 --> 00:43:26.780
This was back--

00:43:26.780 --> 00:43:28.880
2016.

00:43:28.880 --> 00:43:32.510
They would be talking
about TensorFlow, Keras.

00:43:32.510 --> 00:43:36.110
That for me was a
huge black hole.

00:43:36.110 --> 00:43:40.130
I got very limited
value out of that stuff.

00:43:40.130 --> 00:43:45.110
True story-- I built a
regression model for my company

00:43:45.110 --> 00:43:46.460
that ended up--

00:43:46.460 --> 00:43:50.030
honestly, no joke-- helping me
take the part of the company

00:43:50.030 --> 00:43:53.480
that I was overseeing from
about 3 million to 15 million

00:43:53.480 --> 00:43:54.285
in revenue.

00:43:54.285 --> 00:43:55.910
And it was a small
part of the company,

00:43:55.910 --> 00:43:59.640
because the company made about
$500 million a year in revenue.

00:43:59.640 --> 00:44:01.730
So this was kind of
a drop in the bucket.

00:44:01.730 --> 00:44:07.020
But my boss all that, and
my boss's boss saw that,

00:44:07.020 --> 00:44:10.680
and they were like, oh,
wow, this is really pretty

00:44:10.680 --> 00:44:13.290
impressive what you're doing.

00:44:13.290 --> 00:44:18.040
I implemented a lead scoring
algorithm that literally

00:44:18.040 --> 00:44:20.500
was just a logistic regression.

00:44:20.500 --> 00:44:24.540
Now, true story-- then I was
getting good results with that,

00:44:24.540 --> 00:44:27.460
so I was like, oh, this
deep learning stuff--

00:44:27.460 --> 00:44:30.730
this is pretty cool, right?

00:44:30.730 --> 00:44:33.390
I was seeing what people
were doing online with it.

00:44:33.390 --> 00:44:36.690
And they were like, oh, my gosh,
I can do image classification.

00:44:36.690 --> 00:44:38.340
I can do this and that.

00:44:38.340 --> 00:44:40.350
So I hauled off, and
I was like, all right,

00:44:40.350 --> 00:44:42.600
I'm going to learn deep
learning, at least the basics,

00:44:42.600 --> 00:44:48.310
and I'm going to try to apply
it to this lead scoring problem.

00:44:48.310 --> 00:44:51.040
And I spent probably
six months on that,

00:44:51.040 --> 00:44:53.870
and I got nothing out of it.

00:44:53.870 --> 00:44:56.920
My logistic regression
model was better.

00:44:56.920 --> 00:44:59.410
Just trying to
understand TensorFlow,

00:44:59.410 --> 00:45:02.098
and then realizing
TensorFlow's too complicated,

00:45:02.098 --> 00:45:03.640
so then I'm going
to switch to Keras.

00:45:03.640 --> 00:45:06.093
And then, oh, somebody's
talking about PyTorch.

00:45:06.093 --> 00:45:07.510
Actually, I can't
even if remember

00:45:07.510 --> 00:45:08.860
PyTorch was out back then.

00:45:08.860 --> 00:45:14.341
But it's just the process
of seeing all of these--

00:45:14.341 --> 00:45:16.060
I call them red herrings.

00:45:16.060 --> 00:45:19.840
They're these shiny objects out
there that are trying to steal

00:45:19.840 --> 00:45:23.050
your attention, and it's taking
you away from the thing that

00:45:23.050 --> 00:45:23.770
you're--

00:45:23.770 --> 00:45:25.940
that's actually
giving you the value.

00:45:25.940 --> 00:45:29.590
So data wrangling, ggplot--

00:45:29.590 --> 00:45:30.520
work on those.

00:45:30.520 --> 00:45:31.690
Get the foundations.

00:45:31.690 --> 00:45:35.680
Get the basics done,
and get the quick wins,

00:45:35.680 --> 00:45:39.700
and then work your
way up as you go.

00:45:39.700 --> 00:45:42.430
Then machine learning ended
up being really powerful

00:45:42.430 --> 00:45:44.690
for me, and some other stuff.

00:45:44.690 --> 00:45:46.135
So I hope that helps.

00:45:49.285 --> 00:45:52.160
Thank you, Matt, for
doing the numbers thing too,

00:45:52.160 --> 00:45:54.830
because I think it's
always helpful to just see

00:45:54.830 --> 00:45:57.830
how many other people
are feeling that as well.

00:45:57.830 --> 00:46:00.230
Yep, yep.

00:46:00.230 --> 00:46:01.490
Imposter syndrome's a big one.

00:46:01.490 --> 00:46:04.895
Even my students,
a lot of them--

00:46:04.895 --> 00:46:08.180
that can be, honestly,
the sole reason that they

00:46:08.180 --> 00:46:14.360
jump into my program, is because
whatever they're taking, they--

00:46:14.360 --> 00:46:16.910
it's just not
getting them through.

00:46:16.910 --> 00:46:18.295
They always feel every--

00:46:18.295 --> 00:46:20.910
for two steps forward, they're
taking one step backwards.

00:46:20.910 --> 00:46:23.140
So you hate to see that.

00:46:23.140 --> 00:46:25.690
And that's something
I'm on a mission

00:46:25.690 --> 00:46:29.460
to help my students at
least get through that.

00:46:29.460 --> 00:46:31.410
Definitely.

00:46:31.410 --> 00:46:35.790
Siavash, I see you had
your hand raised too.

00:46:35.790 --> 00:46:37.560
I know Siavash.

00:46:37.560 --> 00:46:38.830
How's it going, man?

00:46:38.830 --> 00:46:39.330
Hi, Matt.

00:46:39.330 --> 00:46:41.130
I'm a student,
and proud student.

00:46:41.130 --> 00:46:43.578
I'm really enjoying
the time series course.

00:46:43.578 --> 00:46:44.370
MATT DANCHO: Great.

00:46:44.370 --> 00:46:46.890
SIAVASH SOLEYMANI: I really
thought that it was a--

00:46:46.890 --> 00:46:48.610
I'm already doing a
lot of time series,

00:46:48.610 --> 00:46:50.760
so it's going to
be a quick course.

00:46:50.760 --> 00:46:54.240
But it's a lot of
functions that I struggled

00:46:54.240 --> 00:46:58.140
and I spent a lot of
time to just write them.

00:46:58.140 --> 00:47:01.090
Some of these helper functions
make my life much easier.

00:47:01.090 --> 00:47:04.920
And I'm still not throughout the
course, so I appreciate that.

00:47:04.920 --> 00:47:08.560
My question is, what is the next
big thing in Business Science?

00:47:08.560 --> 00:47:10.950
I'm very looking forward--
what you're working on

00:47:10.950 --> 00:47:14.250
and when it will be coming.

00:47:14.250 --> 00:47:17.260
So I'm working on two
big things right now.

00:47:17.260 --> 00:47:26.988
The first one is actually a
program that combines-- so

00:47:26.988 --> 00:47:29.280
for those of you who don't
know how Business Science is

00:47:29.280 --> 00:47:30.947
structured, I have a
core product, which

00:47:30.947 --> 00:47:32.970
is called the 5 Course R-Track.

00:47:32.970 --> 00:47:35.910
The time series
course that is taking

00:47:35.910 --> 00:47:38.200
is the third course
in that system.

00:47:38.200 --> 00:47:41.370
And it's basically
designed to take you

00:47:41.370 --> 00:47:46.320
from wherever you are now to
being super productive, being

00:47:46.320 --> 00:47:48.990
able to outcompete all
of your competition

00:47:48.990 --> 00:47:52.333
if you're trying to get a job,
or if you already have a job

00:47:52.333 --> 00:47:54.000
and you're trying to
apply data science,

00:47:54.000 --> 00:47:56.130
give you all the
skills necessary--

00:47:56.130 --> 00:47:59.610
things like Shiny, things
like the data wrangling,

00:47:59.610 --> 00:48:01.110
the visualization.

00:48:01.110 --> 00:48:03.750
Time series, which
was a huge win for me

00:48:03.750 --> 00:48:08.760
when I was working back in
the day for Bonney Forge,

00:48:08.760 --> 00:48:14.280
but the next big thing
is to take that system

00:48:14.280 --> 00:48:16.740
and add on an
additional course to it.

00:48:16.740 --> 00:48:22.290
But also, the
additional course is

00:48:22.290 --> 00:48:26.040
to help get you a data
science job in 30 days.

00:48:26.040 --> 00:48:28.680
And then the whole
package is designed

00:48:28.680 --> 00:48:32.640
to also add in a
personalized coach

00:48:32.640 --> 00:48:35.310
that takes you through
the entire program

00:48:35.310 --> 00:48:39.810
in under 180 days, along
with a whole group that goes

00:48:39.810 --> 00:48:41.740
through the process with you.

00:48:41.740 --> 00:48:44.790
So one of the big hurdles
that I was finding

00:48:44.790 --> 00:48:46.870
is that some people--

00:48:46.870 --> 00:48:51.300
like Siavash, you're
highly self-motivated

00:48:51.300 --> 00:48:53.250
and you're crushing the courses.

00:48:53.250 --> 00:48:56.910
But these courses-- I
mean, they're pretty dense,

00:48:56.910 --> 00:49:00.480
because they go deep into how
to solve the business problems

00:49:00.480 --> 00:49:01.695
and get--

00:49:01.695 --> 00:49:03.570
and that's where you
get the value out of it.

00:49:03.570 --> 00:49:07.530
But it's very easy for
life or a curveball

00:49:07.530 --> 00:49:12.180
to come, and to get sidetracked
and then stay unmotivated.

00:49:12.180 --> 00:49:14.730
So we want to have a coach that
helps take you through that

00:49:14.730 --> 00:49:18.270
whole process, somebody who's--
and it's designed for people

00:49:18.270 --> 00:49:20.370
who want to get data
science positions.

00:49:20.370 --> 00:49:23.700
So that way, whether they want
to get a senior data science

00:49:23.700 --> 00:49:27.600
position through
the beta program--

00:49:27.600 --> 00:49:33.360
I've already had one student
get a job at Air Canada.

00:49:33.360 --> 00:49:38.520
She got it in four months
using this program.

00:49:38.520 --> 00:49:42.180
Another student,
she's 22 years old.

00:49:42.180 --> 00:49:44.730
Her name is [? Habi. ?]
I actually hired her

00:49:44.730 --> 00:49:49.260
on as my next coach because
she is so fantastically

00:49:49.260 --> 00:49:51.120
good at getting a data
science interview,

00:49:51.120 --> 00:49:53.430
it's disgustingly scary.

00:49:53.430 --> 00:49:57.060
But she has all the
tricks in the book,

00:49:57.060 --> 00:50:01.620
and she knows how to take
that, the curriculum--

00:50:01.620 --> 00:50:03.630
you guys have to hear this.

00:50:03.630 --> 00:50:09.210
She had no prior data
science experience,

00:50:09.210 --> 00:50:11.550
working as a data scientist.

00:50:11.550 --> 00:50:13.980
She had no prior
working experience other

00:50:13.980 --> 00:50:20.040
than she used to sell wine
for her dad's business.

00:50:20.040 --> 00:50:23.490
Her dad owned a company
that distributed wine.

00:50:23.490 --> 00:50:29.190
So she was able to, in seven
months with this program,

00:50:29.190 --> 00:50:31.600
be able to get her
first data science job--

00:50:31.600 --> 00:50:36.120
she just landed it here
about two weeks ago--

00:50:36.120 --> 00:50:41.560
at the Federal Reserve in
Minneapolis, Minnesota.

00:50:41.560 --> 00:50:44.110
So she crushed it.

00:50:44.110 --> 00:50:47.700
And I had to hire her on
because she had everything

00:50:47.700 --> 00:50:49.590
stacked against her--

00:50:49.590 --> 00:50:52.830
no working experience
prior to that.

00:50:52.830 --> 00:50:55.297
So that's the first thing
that I'm working on.

00:50:55.297 --> 00:50:56.880
We're getting ready
to productionalize

00:50:56.880 --> 00:51:01.550
that because I have to have a
sales team to help sell that.

00:51:01.550 --> 00:51:06.120
It's a higher ticket product
because of the coaching aspect,

00:51:06.120 --> 00:51:09.270
but the results that
it gets are amazing.

00:51:09.270 --> 00:51:12.580
So to productionalize that just
takes a little bit more work.

00:51:12.580 --> 00:51:15.270
So we're probably going to be
launching that here in January.

00:51:15.270 --> 00:51:18.720
And then the other thing
that I have in beta right now

00:51:18.720 --> 00:51:22.170
is I'm leveraging--

00:51:22.170 --> 00:51:25.440
I always seek my
past for guidance,

00:51:25.440 --> 00:51:28.440
and one of the things
I'm doing right now

00:51:28.440 --> 00:51:31.800
is with consulting,
teaching people who have--

00:51:31.800 --> 00:51:35.550
who are dangerous with R
to learn how to turn that

00:51:35.550 --> 00:51:38.010
into a business, whether
it's a side hustle

00:51:38.010 --> 00:51:44.790
or actually creating a
scalable business model.

00:51:44.790 --> 00:51:48.300
I'm teaching them how to do
things like find their niche,

00:51:48.300 --> 00:51:52.200
how to develop an
offer that there's

00:51:52.200 --> 00:51:55.950
market demand for
already out there, how

00:51:55.950 --> 00:52:02.100
to create a business, and how
to basically do everything

00:52:02.100 --> 00:52:03.360
from take--

00:52:03.360 --> 00:52:07.920
to generate leads to building
out a sales presentation

00:52:07.920 --> 00:52:09.420
and selling clients.

00:52:09.420 --> 00:52:15.690
And the goal is to land $25,000
plus data science projects,

00:52:15.690 --> 00:52:20.730
and then to be able to
scale that, so that way

00:52:20.730 --> 00:52:22.860
what they do--

00:52:22.860 --> 00:52:26.400
their first client pays
them to build it, and then

00:52:26.400 --> 00:52:28.620
their next client
they shrink the time,

00:52:28.620 --> 00:52:31.500
because they've already got most
of the code written already,

00:52:31.500 --> 00:52:32.850
and they can reproduce it.

00:52:32.850 --> 00:52:35.730
And it becomes a
scalable business model.

00:52:35.730 --> 00:52:39.070
So that's the next project
I'm working on right now.

00:52:39.070 --> 00:52:41.610
I have nine students
in that beta program.

00:52:41.610 --> 00:52:45.330
And we're just through the
first week of it right now,

00:52:45.330 --> 00:52:47.490
but it's going
really well so far.

00:52:47.490 --> 00:52:49.110
So cross my fingers--

00:52:49.110 --> 00:52:52.635
I'm hoping to launch that
probably mid-next year.

00:52:52.635 --> 00:52:53.760
SIAVASH SOLEYMANI: Awesome.

00:52:53.760 --> 00:52:55.025
Thank you very much.

