either like to learn,

00:53:35.840 --> 00:53:39.050
or how they learn, and
how you use that to change

00:53:39.050 --> 00:53:40.562
how you create courses.

00:53:40.562 --> 00:53:42.020
One of the things
that pops to mind

00:53:42.020 --> 00:53:45.320
is, what is the
data science topic

00:53:45.320 --> 00:53:49.160
that we 2x playback
speed through the most

00:53:49.160 --> 00:53:51.710
frequently, anything like that?

00:53:51.710 --> 00:53:52.580
Yeah.

00:53:52.580 --> 00:53:57.800
So I find learners are either
2x learners, 1.5x learners,

00:53:57.800 --> 00:53:59.300
or 1x learners.

00:53:59.300 --> 00:54:01.790
Once they get the
hang of how to do 2x,

00:54:01.790 --> 00:54:05.490
it's like you're never
going to go back, right?

00:54:05.490 --> 00:54:08.430
I do at least 1 and 1/2x on
all of my YouTube videos.

00:54:08.430 --> 00:54:12.770
That's how I crush through a
lot of content a lot of times.

00:54:12.770 --> 00:54:17.870
And I find that a lot of
learners, when it comes

00:54:17.870 --> 00:54:22.410
to analyzing how they learn--

00:54:22.410 --> 00:54:25.500
so one of the biggest metrics
that I started out with

00:54:25.500 --> 00:54:31.900
was understanding if
my courses were better

00:54:31.900 --> 00:54:34.430
than the rest of the market.

00:54:34.430 --> 00:54:39.910
So I pride myself on as--
just as an online format,

00:54:39.910 --> 00:54:44.300
it is insanely tough to even
get people to take your course.

00:54:44.300 --> 00:54:49.920
So couple of things
we do differently--

00:54:49.920 --> 00:54:51.920
I think the Slack
channel with the support,

00:54:51.920 --> 00:54:53.120
getting the support--

00:54:53.120 --> 00:54:58.250
as soon as they have a problem,
they can hop in there, get an--

00:54:58.250 --> 00:55:02.060
ask any question-- no
question's off the table--

00:55:02.060 --> 00:55:06.020
and really get feedback
at the speed of learning.

00:55:06.020 --> 00:55:08.210
I think that's
incredibly important.

00:55:08.210 --> 00:55:14.300
When I implemented that, really
helped take my completion rates

00:55:14.300 --> 00:55:14.960
up.

00:55:14.960 --> 00:55:16.860
Now, the other thing--

00:55:16.860 --> 00:55:20.960
so having a community
like that really helps.

00:55:20.960 --> 00:55:22.670
Keeping videos small--

00:55:22.670 --> 00:55:24.870
I think that's super critical.

00:55:24.870 --> 00:55:27.435
So there's this thing.

00:55:27.435 --> 00:55:29.060
I can't remember the
name of the curve,

00:55:29.060 --> 00:55:32.850
but people learn in a--
it's a U-shaped curve.

00:55:32.850 --> 00:55:36.200
So if you do an hour
webinar, people only

00:55:36.200 --> 00:55:38.935
remember the first part
of it and the end of it.

00:55:38.935 --> 00:55:40.310
In the middle,
they're only going

00:55:40.310 --> 00:55:42.440
to-- their chances
of remembering

00:55:42.440 --> 00:55:44.180
that-- it's like 3% to 5%.

00:55:44.180 --> 00:55:46.017
But at the beginning
and end-- so

00:55:46.017 --> 00:55:48.350
if you can shrink that time
down-- and one of the things

00:55:48.350 --> 00:55:52.833
I did was I try to keep no
video longer than five minutes.

00:55:52.833 --> 00:55:55.250
So you're going to at least
remember the beginning and end

00:55:55.250 --> 00:55:56.555
of each of those videos.

00:55:56.555 --> 00:55:58.055
And then it's also
going to be M&amp;amp;Ms.

00:55:58.055 --> 00:56:00.560
They're just going to
pop one after the other,

00:56:00.560 --> 00:56:05.060
rather than having to eat
a whole big pile at once.

00:56:05.060 --> 00:56:08.090
So those are some
of the things that I

00:56:08.090 --> 00:56:10.340
started implementing
that really I saw

00:56:10.340 --> 00:56:13.100
move the needle for my program.

00:56:13.100 --> 00:56:15.440
A lot of other stuff
that we do differently,

00:56:15.440 --> 00:56:17.450
starting off with a
business problem first,

00:56:17.450 --> 00:56:20.030
I think that's super critical.

00:56:20.030 --> 00:56:22.970
Getting them a quick win,
showing them a jumpstart--

00:56:22.970 --> 00:56:26.060
not a full analysis, but a
bite-size analysis so they

00:56:26.060 --> 00:56:27.860
can begin with the end in mind.

00:56:27.860 --> 00:56:30.440
They can see and visualize
how this course is

00:56:30.440 --> 00:56:33.470
going to play out and how
that value is going to stack.

00:56:33.470 --> 00:56:36.650
And then by the
end of it, they're

00:56:36.650 --> 00:56:41.330
beyond thrilled because they
saw just at the beginning what

00:56:41.330 --> 00:56:44.120
they're going to accomplish.

00:56:44.120 --> 00:56:46.965
That's funny
about the jumpstart.

00:56:46.965 --> 00:56:49.550
I felt like I had
learned the whole class

00:56:49.550 --> 00:56:50.960
after the jumpstart.

00:56:50.960 --> 00:56:51.710
I got my value.

00:56:51.710 --> 00:56:53.870
And then it's just
putting in the pieces

00:56:53.870 --> 00:56:55.340
and going just a
little bit deeper

00:56:55.340 --> 00:56:56.423
with some of the concepts.

00:56:56.423 --> 00:56:56.970
Yeah.

00:56:56.970 --> 00:56:58.850
So once you get
through the jumpstart,

00:56:58.850 --> 00:57:02.060
you already-- you know
where you're going.

00:57:02.060 --> 00:57:05.090
It's just, how do I get there?

00:57:05.090 --> 00:57:07.850
A lot of it's muscle
memory, repetition

00:57:07.850 --> 00:57:11.060
with some of those
functions, and just

00:57:11.060 --> 00:57:16.880
